{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Supermarket Customer Analytics & Product Recommendation System\n",
        "\n",
        "**Unsupervised Machine Learning | Customer Segmentation | Recommendation System**\n",
        "\n",
        "This project analyzes supermarket transaction behavior, segments customers using K-Means clustering, and builds a product recommendation system using item-based cosine similarity.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Project workflow\n",
        "1. Data understanding and quality checks\n",
        "2. Exploratory data analysis\n",
        "3. Customer-level feature engineering\n",
        "4. K-Means customer segmentation\n",
        "5. PCA visualization and ARI validation\n",
        "6. Customer category preferences\n",
        "7. Item-based product recommendation\n",
        "8. Time-based train/test evaluation\n",
        "9. Recommendation baselines and model comparison\n",
        "10. Business interpretation and conclusion\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 1. Problem Statement\n",
        "\n",
        "A supermarket has a large number of transaction records but needs to understand **who its customers are, how they behave, and which products can be recommended to them**.\n",
        "\n",
        "The project therefore addresses two related business questions:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Can customers be grouped into meaningful behavioral segments without using a supervised target?\n",
        "- Can a customer's previous purchase behavior be used to recommend products they may buy next?\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 2. Objective\n",
        "\n",
        "- Understand transaction and customer purchasing behavior.\n",
        "- Create customer-level behavioral features.\n",
        "- Segment customers using **K-Means clustering**.\n",
        "- Visualize the segments using **PCA**.\n",
        "- Validate the discovered clusters against the dataset's existing `true_segment` field using **Adjusted Rand Index (ARI)**.\n",
        "- Build an item-based recommendation system using **cosine similarity**.\n",
        "- Evaluate recommendations using **Precision@K** and **Recall@K**.\n",
        "- Compare personalized recommenders with a popularity baseline.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 3. Dataset Overview\n",
        "\n",
        "The project dataset contains supermarket transaction-level records.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Observed dataset size:** 527,226 transactions and 20 columns.\n",
        "\n",
        "Important fields include:\n",
        "\n",
        "- `customer_id` \u2014 customer identifier\n",
        "- `true_segment` \u2014 existing customer segment label used only for validation\n",
        "- `age`, `gender`, `monthly_income`, `household_size` \u2014 customer attributes\n",
        "- `invoice_no`, `visit_number`, `purchase_date` \u2014 transaction/visit information\n",
        "- `product_category`, `product` \u2014 purchased product information\n",
        "- `quantity`, `original_unit_price`, `discount_pct`, `unit_price`, `total_amount` \u2014 purchase value information\n",
        "- `month`, `day_of_week`, `purchase_hour`, `used_discount` \u2014 behavioral/time features\n",
        "\n",
        "The source analysis found **no missing values and no duplicate rows**. The dataset contains **5,000 unique customers and 50 unique products**.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 4. Import Libraries"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "import seaborn as sns\n",
        "\n",
        "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
        "from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\n",
        "from sklearn.decomposition import PCA\n",
        "from sklearn.metrics import silhouette_score, adjusted_rand_score\n",
        "from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\n",
        "\n",
        "pd.set_option(\"display.max_columns\", None)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 5. Load Dataset"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Upload smart_supermarket_customer_behavior.csv to the notebook environment first\n",
        "df = pd.read_csv(\"smart_supermarket_customer_behavior.csv\")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Rows, Columns:\", df.shape)\n",
        "display(df.head())\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Output\n",
        "\n",
        "- Shape: **(527226, 20)**\n",
        "- The dataset contains transaction-level supermarket purchase records.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 6. Data Quality Check"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "print(\"Missing values:\")\n",
        "display(df.isnull().sum())\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Duplicate rows:\", df.duplicated().sum())\n",
        "\n",
        "df[\"purchase_date\"] = pd.to_datetime(df[\"purchase_date\"])\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Purchase period:\")\n",
        "print(\"From:\", df[\"purchase_date\"].min())\n",
        "print(\"To  :\", df[\"purchase_date\"].max())\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Unique customers:\", df[\"customer_id\"].nunique())\n",
        "print(\"Unique invoices:\", df[\"invoice_no\"].nunique())\n",
        "print(\"Unique products:\", df[\"product\"].nunique())\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Output\n",
        "\n",
        "- Missing values: **0 across the dataset**\n",
        "- Duplicate rows: **0**\n",
        "- Purchase period: **2025-01-01 to 2025-12-27**\n",
        "- Unique customers: **5,000**\n",
        "- Unique invoices: **68,034**\n",
        "- Unique products: **50**\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 7. Exploratory Data Analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "The transaction data is explored through sales distribution, category sales, product sales, customer spending, shopping hours, discount behavior, and numerical correlations.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Transaction amount distribution\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))\n",
        "sns.histplot(df[\"total_amount\"], bins=50, kde=True)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Distribution of Transaction Amount\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Transaction Amount\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Number of Transactions\")\n",
        "plt.show()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Category sales\n",
        "category_sales = (\n",
        "    df.groupby(\"product_category\")[\"total_amount\"]\n",
        "      .sum()\n",
        "      .sort_values(ascending=False)\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))\n",
        "sns.barplot(x=category_sales.values, y=category_sales.index)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Total Sales by Product Category\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Total Sales\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Product Category\")\n",
        "plt.show()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Top products\n",
        "product_sales = (\n",
        "    df.groupby(\"product\")[\"total_amount\"]\n",
        "      .sum()\n",
        "      .sort_values(ascending=False)\n",
        "      .head(15)\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
        "sns.barplot(x=product_sales.values, y=product_sales.index)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Top 15 Products by Total Sales\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Total Sales\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Product\")\n",
        "plt.show()\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 8. Customer-Level Feature Engineering\n",
        "\n",
        "Transaction-level records are aggregated to one row per customer.\n",
        "\n",
        "The resulting features capture:\n",
        "- spending\n",
        "- visit frequency\n",
        "- quantity purchased\n",
        "- average transaction value\n",
        "- discount behavior\n",
        "- product/category diversity\n",
        "- shopping time\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "customer_features = df.groupby(\"customer_id\").agg(\n",
        "    age=(\"age\", \"first\"),\n",
        "    monthly_income=(\"monthly_income\", \"first\"),\n",
        "    household_size=(\"household_size\", \"first\"),\n",
        "    total_spend=(\"total_amount\", \"sum\"),\n",
        "    total_visits=(\"invoice_no\", \"nunique\"),\n",
        "    total_items=(\"quantity\", \"sum\"),\n",
        "    avg_transaction_value=(\"total_amount\", \"mean\"),\n",
        "    avg_discount=(\"discount_pct\", \"mean\"),\n",
        "    unique_products=(\"product\", \"nunique\"),\n",
        "    unique_categories=(\"product_category\", \"nunique\"),\n",
        "    avg_purchase_hour=(\"purchase_hour\", \"mean\")\n",
        ").reset_index()\n",
        "\n",
        "customer_features[\"spend_per_visit\"] = (\n",
        "    customer_features[\"total_spend\"] / customer_features[\"total_visits\"]\n",
        ")\n",
        "customer_features[\"items_per_visit\"] = (\n",
        "    customer_features[\"total_items\"] / customer_features[\"total_visits\"]\n",
        ")\n",
        "customer_features[\"spend_per_item\"] = (\n",
        "    customer_features[\"total_spend\"] / customer_features[\"total_items\"]\n",
        ")\n",
        "customer_features[\"products_per_category\"] = (\n",
        "    customer_features[\"unique_products\"] / customer_features[\"unique_categories\"]\n",
        ")\n",
        "customer_features[\"spend_to_income_ratio\"] = (\n",
        "    customer_features[\"total_spend\"] /\n",
        "    (customer_features[\"monthly_income\"] * 12)\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Customer feature dataset shape:\", customer_features.shape)\n",
        "display(customer_features.head())\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Output\n",
        "\n",
        "Customer feature dataset shape: **(5000, 16+ customer identifier column)**.\n",
        "\n",
        "The source analysis confirmed **5,000 customers, no missing feature values, and no duplicate customer IDs**.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 9. Prepare Features for K-Means\n",
        "\n",
        "K-Means is distance-based, so the customer features are standardized before clustering.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "clustering_features = [\n",
        "    \"age\", \"monthly_income\", \"household_size\", \"total_spend\",\n",
        "    \"total_visits\", \"total_items\", \"avg_transaction_value\",\n",
        "    \"avg_discount\", \"unique_products\", \"unique_categories\",\n",
        "    \"avg_purchase_hour\", \"spend_per_visit\", \"items_per_visit\",\n",
        "    \"spend_per_item\", \"products_per_category\", \"spend_to_income_ratio\"\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "X = customer_features[clustering_features]\n",
        "\n",
        "scaler = StandardScaler()\n",
        "X_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Features used:\", len(clustering_features))\n",
        "print(\"Scaled data shape:\", X_scaled.shape)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Output\n",
        "\n",
        "- Features used for clustering: **16**\n",
        "- Scaled data shape: **(5000, 16)**\n",
        "- After standardization, each feature has approximately mean 0 and standard deviation 1.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 10. Select Number of Clusters\n",
        "\n",
        "Both the Elbow Method and Silhouette Score are examined.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "inertia = []\n",
        "silhouette_scores = []\n",
        "K_range = range(2, 11)\n",
        "\n",
        "for k in K_range:\n",
        "    model = KMeans(n_clusters=k, random_state=42, n_init=10)\n",
        "    labels = model.fit_predict(X_scaled)\n",
        "    inertia.append(model.inertia_)\n",
        "    silhouette_scores.append(silhouette_score(X_scaled, labels))\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))\n",
        "plt.plot(K_range, inertia, marker=\"o\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Elbow Method for Optimal Number of Clusters\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"K\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Inertia\")\n",
        "plt.grid(True)\n",
        "plt.show()\n",
        "\n",
        "silhouette_results = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    \"K\": list(K_range),\n",
        "    \"Silhouette_Score\": silhouette_scores\n",
        "})\n",
        "display(silhouette_results)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Output from the original analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "| K | Silhouette Score |\n",
        "|---:|---:|\n",
        "| 2 | 0.3675 |\n",
        "| 3 | 0.3516 |\n",
        "| 4 | 0.2393 |\n",
        "| 5 | 0.2149 |\n",
        "| 6 | 0.2115 |\n",
        "| 7 | 0.1883 |\n",
        "| 8 | 0.1884 |\n",
        "| 9 | 0.1786 |\n",
        "| 10 | 0.1786 |\n",
        "\n",
        "The original project selected **K = 3** for the final business segmentation.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 11. Final K-Means Customer Segmentation"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "final_kmeans = KMeans(\n",
        "    n_clusters=3,\n",
        "    random_state=42,\n",
        "    n_init=10\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "customer_features[\"cluster\"] = final_kmeans.fit_predict(X_scaled)\n",
        "\n",
        "cluster_profile = customer_features.groupby(\"cluster\").agg(\n",
        "    customers=(\"customer_id\", \"count\"),\n",
        "    avg_age=(\"age\", \"mean\"),\n",
        "    avg_income=(\"monthly_income\", \"mean\"),\n",
        "    avg_spend=(\"total_spend\", \"mean\"),\n",
        "    avg_visits=(\"total_visits\", \"mean\"),\n",
        "    avg_items=(\"total_items\", \"mean\"),\n",
        "    avg_transaction=(\"avg_transaction_value\", \"mean\"),\n",
        "    avg_discount=(\"avg_discount\", \"mean\"),\n",
        "    avg_unique_products=(\"unique_products\", \"mean\"),\n",
        "    avg_categories=(\"unique_categories\", \"mean\"),\n",
        "    avg_spend_per_visit=(\"spend_per_visit\", \"mean\"),\n",
        "    avg_items_per_visit=(\"items_per_visit\", \"mean\")\n",
        ").round(2)\n",
        "\n",
        "display(customer_features[\"cluster\"].value_counts().sort_index())\n",
        "display(cluster_profile)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Output\n",
        "\n",
        "| Cluster | Customers | Avg Age | Avg Income | Avg Spend | Avg Visits | Avg Transaction |\n",
        "|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n",
        "| 0 | 1001 | 39.30 | 40,281 | 87,296.33 | 12.10 | 687.61 |\n",
        "| 1 | 3004 | 26.44 | 35,581.61 | 39,486.60 | 15.31 | 410.01 |\n",
        "| 2 | 995 | 42.28 | 124,022 | 73,586.20 | 9.97 | 671.85 |\n",
        "\n",
        "The clusters show clear behavioral differences in income, spending, visits, and transaction value.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 12. PCA Visualization"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "pca = PCA(n_components=2)\n",
        "X_pca = pca.fit_transform(X_scaled)\n",
        "\n",
        "pca_df = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    \"PC1\": X_pca[:, 0],\n",
        "    \"PC2\": X_pca[:, 1],\n",
        "    \"cluster\": customer_features[\"cluster\"]\n",
        "})\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"PC1:\", round(pca.explained_variance_ratio_[0] * 100, 2), \"%\")\n",
        "print(\"PC2:\", round(pca.explained_variance_ratio_[1] * 100, 2), \"%\")\n",
        "print(\"Total:\", round(pca.explained_variance_ratio_.sum() * 100, 2), \"%\")\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
        "sns.scatterplot(data=pca_df, x=\"PC1\", y=\"PC2\", hue=\"cluster\", s=50, alpha=0.7)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Customer Segments using PCA\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Principal Component 1\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Principal Component 2\")\n",
        "plt.grid(True)\n",
        "plt.show()\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Output\n",
        "\n",
        "- PC1 explained variance: **43.58%**\n",
        "- PC2 explained variance: **20.30%**\n",
        "- Combined explained variance: **63.89%**\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 13. Validate Segmentation with Adjusted Rand Index\n",
        "\n",
        "The dataset contains an existing `true_segment` field. It is **not used to train K-Means**; it is used only as an external validation reference.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "true_labels = df.groupby(\"customer_id\")[\"true_segment\"].first()\n",
        "\n",
        "validation_df = customer_features[[\"customer_id\", \"cluster\"]].copy()\n",
        "validation_df[\"true_segment\"] = validation_df[\"customer_id\"].map(true_labels)\n",
        "\n",
        "ari_score = adjusted_rand_score(\n",
        "    validation_df[\"true_segment\"],\n",
        "    validation_df[\"cluster\"]\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Adjusted Rand Index (ARI):\", round(ari_score, 4))\n",
        "\n",
        "cluster_vs_true = pd.crosstab(\n",
        "    validation_df[\"cluster\"],\n",
        "    validation_df[\"true_segment\"]\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "display(cluster_vs_true)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Output\n",
        "\n",
        "**Adjusted Rand Index (ARI): 0.4778**\n",
        "\n",
        "The clustering captures meaningful structure in the customer data, although the unsupervised clusters do not perfectly reproduce all five predefined segment labels.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 14. Customer Product Preferences\n",
        "\n",
        "A customer-category spending matrix is created to understand which categories dominate each customer's purchasing behavior.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "customer_category = pd.pivot_table(\n",
        "    df,\n",
        "    index=\"customer_id\",\n",
        "    columns=\"product_category\",\n",
        "    values=\"total_amount\",\n",
        "    aggfunc=\"sum\",\n",
        "    fill_value=0\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "customer_category_pct = customer_category.div(\n",
        "    customer_category.sum(axis=1), axis=0\n",
        ") * 100\n",
        "\n",
        "customer_category_pct[\"dominant_category\"] = (\n",
        "    customer_category_pct.drop(columns=\"dominant_category\", errors=\"ignore\").idxmax(axis=1)\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "display(customer_category_pct[\"dominant_category\"].value_counts())\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Output\n",
        "\n",
        "The most common primary category preferences were:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Personal Care: **2,262 customers**\n",
        "- Household: **1,398**\n",
        "- Bakery: **632**\n",
        "- Instant Food: **450**\n",
        "- Staples: **100**\n",
        "\n",
        "This provides a useful bridge between customer segmentation and product recommendation.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 15. Build Customer-Product Matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "For recommendation, each customer is represented by their spending across the 50 products.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "customer_product = pd.pivot_table(\n",
        "    df,\n",
        "    index=\"customer_id\",\n",
        "    columns=\"product\",\n",
        "    values=\"total_amount\",\n",
        "    aggfunc=\"sum\",\n",
        "    fill_value=0\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Customer-Product Matrix Shape:\", customer_product.shape)\n",
        "display(customer_product.head())\n",
        "\n",
        "product_similarity = cosine_similarity(customer_product.T)\n",
        "\n",
        "product_similarity_df = pd.DataFrame(\n",
        "    product_similarity,\n",
        "    index=customer_product.columns,\n",
        "    columns=customer_product.columns\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Product Similarity Matrix Shape:\", product_similarity_df.shape)\n",
        "display(product_similarity_df.head())\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Output\n",
        "\n",
        "- Customer-Product Matrix: **(5000, 50)**\n",
        "- Product Similarity Matrix: **(50, 50)**\n",
        "\n",
        "Cosine similarity measures how similarly products are purchased across customers.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 16. Example: Similar Product Search"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "def get_similar_products(product_name, top_n=5):\n",
        "    if product_name not in product_similarity_df.columns:\n",
        "        print(\"Product not found.\")\n",
        "        return None\n",
        "\n",
        "    similar_products = (\n",
        "        product_similarity_df[product_name]\n",
        "        .drop(product_name)\n",
        "        .sort_values(ascending=False)\n",
        "        .head(top_n)\n",
        "    )\n",
        "\n",
        "    return pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "        \"Product\": similar_products.index,\n",
        "        \"Similarity_Score\": similar_products.values\n",
        "    }).reset_index(drop=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "display(get_similar_products(\"Apples\", top_n=5))\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Output from the original analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "For **Apples**, the most similar products included:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Grapes \u2014 0.7518\n",
        "2. Mangoes \u2014 0.7503\n",
        "3. Oranges \u2014 0.7490\n",
        "4. Bananas \u2014 0.7472\n",
        "5. Yogurt \u2014 0.6233\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 17. Time-Based Train/Test Split\n",
        "\n",
        "To avoid using future purchases to recommend past purchases, the last visit of every customer is held out as test data.\n",
        "\n",
        "This gives:\n",
        "- Training transactions: **486,615**\n",
        "- Test transactions: **40,611**\n",
        "- Customers represented in both sets: **5,000**\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "df_sorted = df.sort_values(\n",
        "    [\"customer_id\", \"purchase_date\"]\n",
        ").copy()\n",
        "\n",
        "last_visit = df_sorted.groupby(\"customer_id\")[\"invoice_no\"].transform(\"max\")\n",
        "\n",
        "test_df = df_sorted[df_sorted[\"invoice_no\"] == last_visit].copy()\n",
        "train_df = df_sorted[df_sorted[\"invoice_no\"] != last_visit].copy()\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Training transactions:\", train_df.shape)\n",
        "print(\"Test transactions:\", test_df.shape)\n",
        "print(\"Customers in train:\", train_df[\"customer_id\"].nunique())\n",
        "print(\"Customers in test:\", test_df[\"customer_id\"].nunique())\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 18. V1 \u2014 Item-Similarity Recommendation\n",
        "\n",
        "For each customer:\n",
        "1. Find products purchased in training history.\n",
        "2. Find products similar to those products.\n",
        "3. Exclude already purchased products.\n",
        "4. Aggregate similarity scores.\n",
        "5. Return the top-N products.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "train_customer_product = pd.pivot_table(\n",
        "    train_df,\n",
        "    index=\"customer_id\",\n",
        "    columns=\"product\",\n",
        "    values=\"total_amount\",\n",
        "    aggfunc=\"sum\",\n",
        "    fill_value=0\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "train_product_similarity = cosine_similarity(train_customer_product.T)\n",
        "\n",
        "train_product_similarity_df = pd.DataFrame(\n",
        "    train_product_similarity,\n",
        "    index=train_customer_product.columns,\n",
        "    columns=train_customer_product.columns\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "def recommend_from_training(customer_id, top_n=10):\n",
        "    purchased_products = train_customer_product.loc[customer_id]\n",
        "    purchased_products = purchased_products[purchased_products > 0].index.tolist()\n",
        "\n",
        "    recommendation_scores = {}\n",
        "\n",
        "    for product in purchased_products:\n",
        "        similar_products = train_product_similarity_df[product].drop(product)\n",
        "\n",
        "        for candidate, score in similar_products.items():\n",
        "            if candidate not in purchased_products:\n",
        "                recommendation_scores[candidate] = (\n",
        "                    recommendation_scores.get(candidate, 0) + score\n",
        "                )\n",
        "\n",
        "    recommendations = (\n",
        "        pd.Series(recommendation_scores)\n",
        "        .sort_values(ascending=False)\n",
        "        .head(top_n)\n",
        "    )\n",
        "\n",
        "    return recommendations.index.tolist()\n",
        "\n",
        "print(recommend_from_training(10001, top_n=10))\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 19. V1 Evaluation\n",
        "\n",
        "Precision@K measures how many recommended items are relevant.\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall@K measures how many of the customer's held-out purchased items were recovered.\n",
        "\n",
        "The last visit of each customer is treated as the ground-truth test set.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "def evaluate_recommendations(top_n=10):\n",
        "    precisions = []\n",
        "    recalls = []\n",
        "\n",
        "    for customer_id in train_customer_product.index:\n",
        "        actual_products = set(\n",
        "            test_df[test_df[\"customer_id\"] == customer_id][\"product\"]\n",
        "        )\n",
        "\n",
        "        recommended_products = set(\n",
        "            recommend_from_training(customer_id, top_n=top_n)\n",
        "        )\n",
        "\n",
        "        if len(actual_products) == 0:\n",
        "            continue\n",
        "\n",
        "        hits = len(actual_products & recommended_products)\n",
        "\n",
        "        precisions.append(hits / top_n)\n",
        "        recalls.append(hits / len(actual_products))\n",
        "\n",
        "    return np.mean(precisions), np.mean(recalls)\n",
        "\n",
        "precision_5, recall_5 = evaluate_recommendations(top_n=5)\n",
        "precision_10, recall_10 = evaluate_recommendations(top_n=10)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Precision@5  : {precision_5:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Recall@5     : {recall_5:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Precision@10 : {precision_10:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Recall@10    : {recall_10:.4f}\")\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### V1 Output\n",
        "\n",
        "| Metric | Score |\n",
        "|---|---:|\n",
        "| Precision@5 | **0.0564** |\n",
        "| Recall@5 | **0.0412** |\n",
        "| Precision@10 | **0.0459** |\n",
        "| Recall@10 | **0.0656** |\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 20. V2 \u2014 Spend-Weighted Similarity\n",
        "\n",
        "V2 gives greater weight to products on which a customer spends more.\n",
        "\n",
        "The idea is: **products that matter more to a customer should contribute more strongly to the recommendation score.**\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "def recommend_v2(customer_id, top_n=10):\n",
        "    customer_history = train_customer_product.loc[customer_id]\n",
        "    customer_history = customer_history[customer_history > 0]\n",
        "\n",
        "    recommendation_scores = {}\n",
        "    total_spend = customer_history.sum()\n",
        "\n",
        "    for product, spend in customer_history.items():\n",
        "        weight = spend / total_spend\n",
        "        similar_products = train_product_similarity_df[product].drop(product)\n",
        "\n",
        "        for candidate, similarity in similar_products.items():\n",
        "            if candidate not in customer_history.index:\n",
        "                score = weight * similarity\n",
        "                recommendation_scores[candidate] = (\n",
        "                    recommendation_scores.get(candidate, 0) + score\n",
        "                )\n",
        "\n",
        "    return (\n",
        "        pd.Series(recommendation_scores)\n",
        "        .sort_values(ascending=False)\n",
        "        .head(top_n)\n",
        "    )\n",
        "\n",
        "def evaluate_v2(top_n=10):\n",
        "    precisions, recalls = [], []\n",
        "\n",
        "    for customer_id in train_customer_product.index:\n",
        "        actual_products = set(\n",
        "            test_df[test_df[\"customer_id\"] == customer_id][\"product\"]\n",
        "        )\n",
        "        recommended_products = set(\n",
        "            recommend_v2(customer_id, top_n=top_n).index\n",
        "        )\n",
        "\n",
        "        if not actual_products:\n",
        "            continue\n",
        "\n",
        "        hits = len(actual_products & recommended_products)\n",
        "        precisions.append(hits / top_n)\n",
        "        recalls.append(hits / len(actual_products))\n",
        "\n",
        "    return np.mean(precisions), np.mean(recalls)\n",
        "\n",
        "precision_5_v2, recall_5_v2 = evaluate_v2(5)\n",
        "precision_10_v2, recall_10_v2 = evaluate_v2(10)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Precision@5  : {precision_5_v2:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Recall@5     : {recall_5_v2:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Precision@10 : {precision_10_v2:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Recall@10    : {recall_10_v2:.4f}\")\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### V2 Output\n",
        "\n",
        "| Metric | Score |\n",
        "|---|---:|\n",
        "| Precision@5 | **0.0559** |\n",
        "| Recall@5 | **0.0405** |\n",
        "| Precision@10 | **0.0461** |\n",
        "| Recall@10 | **0.0655** |\n",
        "\n",
        "V2 did **not** materially improve the overall recommendation quality over V1 in this evaluation.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 21. Popularity Baseline\n",
        "\n",
        "A strong baseline is needed. Instead of personalized recommendations, the popularity model simply recommends the most frequently purchased products.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "product_popularity = (\n",
        "    train_df.groupby(\"product\")[\"quantity\"]\n",
        "    .sum()\n",
        "    .sort_values(ascending=False)\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "display(product_popularity.head(10))\n",
        "\n",
        "def popularity_recommendation(top_n=10):\n",
        "    return product_popularity.head(top_n).index.tolist()\n",
        "\n",
        "def evaluate_popularity(top_n=10):\n",
        "    precisions, recalls = [], []\n",
        "    recommendations = set(popularity_recommendation(top_n))\n",
        "\n",
        "    for customer_id in train_customer_product.index:\n",
        "        actual_products = set(\n",
        "            test_df[test_df[\"customer_id\"] == customer_id][\"product\"]\n",
        "        )\n",
        "\n",
        "        if not actual_products:\n",
        "            continue\n",
        "\n",
        "        hits = len(actual_products & recommendations)\n",
        "        precisions.append(hits / top_n)\n",
        "        recalls.append(hits / len(actual_products))\n",
        "\n",
        "    return np.mean(precisions), np.mean(recalls)\n",
        "\n",
        "precision_5_pop, recall_5_pop = evaluate_popularity(5)\n",
        "precision_10_pop, recall_10_pop = evaluate_popularity(10)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Precision@5  : {precision_5_pop:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Recall@5     : {recall_5_pop:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Precision@10 : {precision_10_pop:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Recall@10    : {recall_10_pop:.4f}\")\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Popularity Baseline Output\n",
        "\n",
        "| Metric | Score |\n",
        "|---|---:|\n",
        "| Precision@5 | **0.2515** |\n",
        "| Recall@5 | **0.1754** |\n",
        "| Precision@10 | **0.2319** |\n",
        "| Recall@10 | **0.3126** |\n",
        "\n",
        "The popularity baseline clearly outperformed V1 and V2 on the current evaluation.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 22. Final Recommendation Insight\n",
        "\n",
        "The experiment shows an important practical lesson:\n",
        "\n",
        "> A more personalized algorithm is not automatically better than a simple baseline.\n",
        "\n",
        "For this dataset and evaluation setup, the popularity model produced substantially higher Precision@K and Recall@K than the item-similarity approaches.\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore, the current evidence supports using the **popularity baseline as the strongest tested recommender**, while keeping the similarity-based approach as a foundation for future hybrid personalization.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 23. Business Interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "### Customer segmentation\n",
        "The three K-Means clusters differ strongly in income, spending, visit frequency, and average transaction value. This can support targeted promotions and customer-specific marketing strategies.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Product recommendation\n",
        "The item-similarity model learns product relationships from customer purchasing patterns. However, the popularity baseline performs better on the held-out last-visit evaluation.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Practical strategy\n",
        "A production system could combine:\n",
        "- popularity\n",
        "- customer-specific purchase history\n",
        "- product similarity\n",
        "- customer segment\n",
        "- category preference\n",
        "- recency/frequency\n",
        "- promotions and inventory information\n",
        "\n",
        "rather than relying on one signal alone.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 24. Conclusion\n",
        "\n",
        "This project developed an end-to-end supermarket analytics workflow using unsupervised learning and recommendation techniques.\n",
        "\n",
        "The analysis started with **527,226 transaction records from 5,000 customers and 50 products**, followed by data quality checks, EDA, customer-level feature engineering, and standardized behavioral features.\n",
        "\n",
        "K-Means produced three interpretable customer groups. PCA represented 63.89% of the standardized feature variance in two dimensions, while the external ARI validation score was 0.4778.\n",
        "\n",
        "For recommendations, item-based cosine similarity was tested in two forms:\n",
        "- V1: similarity-based recommendations\n",
        "- V2: similarity weighted by customer spending\n",
        "\n",
        "A popularity-based recommender was also evaluated as a baseline. On the held-out last-visit evaluation, the popularity baseline achieved the strongest results.\n",
        "\n",
        "The main takeaway is that **model complexity should be justified by evaluation performance**. The next step would be a hybrid recommendation model that combines popularity with personalized similarity, customer segment, category preference, and recency.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 25. Future Improvements\n",
        "\n",
        "- Build a hybrid recommendation model.\n",
        "- Add recency and purchase frequency.\n",
        "- Use customer cluster information in recommendation scoring.\n",
        "- Add category-level personalization.\n",
        "- Experiment with collaborative filtering / matrix factorization.\n",
        "- Tune recommendation weights using validation data.\n",
        "- Evaluate with additional ranking metrics such as MAP@K and NDCG@K.\n",
        "- Add cold-start handling for new customers and new products.\n"
      ]
    }
  ],
